hi I'm Matt Welch editor-in-chief of  Reason magazine and I'm joined today by  William Redpath the chairman of the  Libertarian Party of the United States  of America thanks for joining us since I  have you I got to ask looking back at  2008 how'd that go you hear a lot of  people sort of capital L libertarians  and small l libertarians who expressed  some disappointment that the year of Ron  Paul kind of dissipated into into  quarreling and that's a bob barr who is  sort of a high-profile candidate didn't  do as well as possible what's your take  on should you feel disappointed or happy  about it and and you know how could the  party have done better I think it's  mixed but there's a lot of positive that  happened I mean certainly everybody  wanted Bob Barr to get more votes than  he got I think it's fair to note that he  got three times approximately the number  of votes the Constitution Party  candidate and the green party candidate  he got fewer popular votes by about  200,000 and Ralph Nader but you have to  keep that in perspective I mean Ralph  Nader's name ID is far greater than Bob  bars I mean after all the Atlantic  Monthly Magazine ran an article two or  three years ago naming what they thought  were the 100 most influential people not  living but of all time in the United  States Ralph Nader was on that list at  number 99 and that took me by surprise  actually but but he has that influence  he's had influence no question and after  florida in two thousand and all that  certainly his name ID and our politics  are so name ID driven it really  shouldn't be that much of a surprise  that he pulled more votes than bob barr  but but i think in in many respects i  mean there are people in that campaign  and particularly bob barr himself who  made real sacrifices i think they did  the best the best that they could I  don't think that certainly libertarians  didn't agree with every judgment in the  campaign but you're going to run into  that with every campaign overall I think  they put in a very stalwart effort a  very solid effort to and we all wish  they'd have done better  but and I'm sure Bob Barr would be the  first person to say that but overall I  think it was it was a good campaign and  accomplished a lot with respect to other  offices first of all we came out of 2008  with ballot access and 27 states that is  a record the previous record had been 26  we described quickly we mean by ballot  access well I'm talking about  presidential or essentially statewide  ballot access where we don't have to  petition again to get back on the ballot  important of those states was North  Carolina where they had reduced the very  difficult state for ballot access we had  to get over a hundred thousand total  signatures in order to qualify for the  party for the ballot in 2008 in North  Carolina we had a tremendous candidate  for governor a Michael Munger who's  chairman of the political science  department at Duke University and they  had recently changed the law in North  Carolina not to make it easier to get on  the ballot to make it easier to stay on  the ballot once you were on lowering it  from ten percent of the vote for  president or governor to two percent he  got nearly three percent of the vote  qualifying us for ballot access in North  Carolina as a party for the next four  years and also keeping things in  perspective I mean yes I wish the  Libertarian Party were a greater  electoral success than what it is but  you know given our voting system which  sort of tilts everything toward two  dominant parties both running for the  center trying to offend as few people as  possible so they can win the single  member legislative district elections  but it's very difficult for a third  party in the United States but when you  compare the Libertarian Party in terms  of number of candidates in terms of  number of votes for US Senate for us  house for state Senate for state house  we absolutely cleaned up relative to the  other minor parties in the United States  so to that extent in comparison to our  peer parties and not the two major  parties we had a very successful 2008  you were on the ballot in all 50 states  and I think 1992 1996 and something more  like 45 this time around is it getting  more difficult to get on the ballot well  I think that 50 state ballot access for  the presidential ticket I at one point  in time I thought that certainly the  mainstream press  can't ignore us if our presidential  ticket is on the ballot in all 50 states  and that's when I really got involved in  ballot access in 1990 leading up to the  92 election and and that turned out  unfortunately not to be the case we got  on the ballot in all 50 states with our  presidential ticket and they largely  ignored us anyway I think that it's it's  not as important it's not viewed as  important in the Libertarian Party I  think as it used to be but and there  were some there were some problems that  cropped up in 2008 in terms of execution  of ballot drives that I think that that  we're going to try to avoid in the  future so I actually right now I do hope  our presidential ticket will be back on  the ballot in all 50 states and the  District of Columbia in 2012 at the very  least it should be in the high 40's is  the Tea Party movement and generalized  opposition to kind of Obama's big  government and bailout type of mentality  is that rebounding to libertarian  party's benefits do you see a more  fertile ground for what you you're  trying to do to a certain extent yes I  think we are picking up some people from  those movements but i think that the tea  parties and I spoke at the Tea Party in  Reston Virginia back on april fifteenth  2009 and they are trying not to make it  a minor party movement I think there's a  conscious effort there to not do that so  but but I do think that there are a  number of people who are joining the  party who are becoming exposed to the  party because we have people who are  involved in those Tea Party rallies talk  a little bit about yourself when did you  first become a libertarian whether  capital L or small out how did that  happen it was in the early 1980s I think  I began subscribing to Reason magazine  yes that's I think that's what happened  basically I was in high school whether  it was during the Watergate area I was a  young Democrat and we were totally  harmless don't worry everyone we SAT  around at somebody's house on Sunday  night and talked and we did nothing to  promote government that was effective I  assure you and I think I I think I  became  I sort of affiliated in my mind at least  more with the Republican Party once I  got to college and began studying  economics and then went to business  school and that takes us into the early  80s and I decided that Ronald Reagan was  not serious about cutting the size of  government and it was really  disappointment with Ronald Reagan's  unserious nasai mean he keep I hope he  wasn't the last best hope in terms of  halting or reducing the size of  government maybe but I hope not but he  just was not serious about it and if he  had been I think that he might not have  been elected to a second term but we'd  all be a lot better off today and and  also frankly the prosecution of the drug  war I thought was absolutely outrageous  on his part and and also frankly Social  Security I smelled a rat when it came to  the Social Security system a long time  ago and it was a combination of those  things that led me to believe that the  Republican Party was not serious about  cutting the size of government and there  has been nothing in the intervening  quarter century to get me to change my  mind looking at the current climate  situation Washington in the country even  the world what are your biggest fears  about what might happen and what are  your sort of centers of hope well I  think that ultimately I in terms of  centers of hope I think that ultimately  we I think the world will be better 30  years from today than it is today just  as I think it's better today than it was  30 years ago I think that that basically  in many respects time is on libertarian  side on the on the other hand I'm just I  am very much concerned about certain  policies in the United States  particularly after the election of  barack obama on the negative side I  think demographics are working against  us given that older people and we are an  aging society older people i think are  more interested in security than they  are freedom and i think that in one  sense that is working against  libertarians and the libertarian  movement in the Libertarian Party right  now but on the other hand I do think  that there is a natural progression  that the economy even though we have  problems right now will over the long  run get better there will be more  affluence and and maybe the silver  lining in the crisis is that that some  people have said isn't it don't you get  kind of depressed sometimes and I say  well don't worry we'll have a  libertarian Society someday when it's  imposed on us by the Chinese government  and and by that I mean that I do think  that ultimately that if our politicians  don't have the Kahuna's to step up and  make the tough decisions they mean to  make our foreign creditors are going to  make them for us and I hope it doesn't  come to that but I do think that there  is a natural limit to the growth of  government ultimately and and if  Americans don't decide to impose that on  this nation then like I said I think our  foreign creditors will but we have a  natural limit to our interview time here  thank you very much and for reason TV  I'm Matt Welch  you
 

